> So I personally would like to see both A and D implemented. Option A is very nice for exporting between multiple inline script tags that are hand-written in a page, and Option D would be great for inlining modules from a module graph.

@justinfagnani Sorry I missed this.  Could you explain what you mean by "inlining modules from a module graph"? I think the word "from" is confusing me.  

Are you basically saying that "Option D would allow for inline overrides of modules in a module graph"?  If so, are you opposed to doing this in two steps with what option A would specify (the new resolution rule for `#` prefixed module specifiers) along with the existing import map syntax?

```
<script type="importmap">
  {
    "imports": {
      "foo": "#foo"
    }
  }
</script>

<script id="foo" type="module">
    export const foo = "bar";
</script>

<script type="module">

    // based on the above importmap, the "foo" specifier
    // references the inline module script with id="foo" above
    // and foo === "bar"

    import { foo } from "foo";

</script>

``` 

Basically why I like A is that this update would focus on allowing inline scripts to **participate in module graphs** through a new default resolution rule (that `#` prefixed specifiers are resolved to inline module scripts with that `id`), but overriding specifiers added through inline scripts within a graph would be done just like overriding any specifier with a default resolution rule (or adding a specifier that doesn't have a default rule): through import maps.
